#{[#uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087459 :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "8973fb88-5098-5e4b-a0b0-25638565dd93" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a11d5a1d-e93d-5a5d-8111-e33d997e2841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087276} {:answer/id #uuid "e572ebc9-141f-578b-93ec-080a0deeb913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y \\cdot 2x) = 2x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087277} {:answer/id #uuid "fd667700-3e8d-53d0-9c71-264753a1f8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{2}) = x^{2} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087278} {:answer/id #uuid "6e285ba0-0b5f-5e5a-a7bd-e5b3de551a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{2}}) = e^{x^{2}} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087279} {:answer/id #uuid "76e91a95-c637-5848-86e4-e944f3eee17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087280}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8fe340-5770-5791-befc-e802db4415ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087275}] :question/id #uuid "8973fb88-5098-5e4b-a0b0-25638565dd93" :question/math-academy-id "q-8546" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy = 2xe^{-x^{2}}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."}] [#uuid "8d7eb342-85c3-52e0-b57f-d62a1be729ce" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c13a4f93-e1f9-5884-a39c-c79a4bcb3e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x\\frac{dy}{dx} - x^{2}y = x\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084555} {:answer/id #uuid "c3444d68-c1e5-5947-a906-9f7391cdd707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} - \\frac{e^{x}}{x}y = x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084556} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b4eab8-6e07-5ae7-96d3-a9034a47b11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 4xy = \\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084557} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0bf6cc-cd01-5352-adcb-7f989d4f9813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} - x^{2} = \\frac{1}{1 - y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084558} {:answer/id #uuid "511a9ab3-97fa-5de3-a077-f939d6fb35ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} - (\\tan x)y = \\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084559}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0c68cfab-095b-58cc-9da7-ba0916b77f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084554}] :question/id #uuid "8d7eb342-85c3-52e0-b57f-d62a1be729ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-103560" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first order linear ordinary differential equation?"}] [#uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096416 :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right)\\\n                                                  &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( 3e^x\\right) +  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\ln 3 \\right)\\\n                                                  &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0\\\n                                                  &= 3 e^x.\n}\n$$"}] [#uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096407 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d56ade3-8c2b-558b-8dee-d6c16408ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=2x-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348568} {:answer/id #uuid "19d75fc4-d565-5247-a3d2-0dd1d71481b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348669} {:answer/id #uuid "33f582cb-d125-5a7e-bc13-6fd636ea22e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348919} {:answer/id #uuid "a15aa42d-fc0c-537c-988d-61613c47dc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349885} {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ee4a69-5c37-5b5b-9d83-fd6eb1f2f193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"}] [#uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166658 :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [#uuid "a33d5262-3afa-580b-b512-aba7c2576e69" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e8c57d-66b6-54a2-a409-f04037ac2dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/8b/8bd666d1415f83ff8232e39963248ef0afe62f39e66d5f3416ae18316453faa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392716} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7c9c8c-a18a-575b-bbc5-e03ae2420c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/5d/5d686380cd025e83e38524918c5cfaf215726f0f0d05783b8f88fd5f281c091c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392717} {:answer/id #uuid "f007b1ae-80cd-5a65-860b-3cf4ed12c156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/0a/0ab0b12088ac6816f7086e952e749ea6d4c9107cc6189ebb0efcafb904152ad0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392718} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ff690e-b86c-57a2-9518-abe046479e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/65/65ae1770ff4084d5a89fdda0f552f2a92380ae948c2b0f1ef3d3bb5d20f32713.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392719} {:answer/id #uuid "06d4b073-7af6-56cc-939c-5a4f1e989fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/1a/1a0e6d6d025e2f8b79019036637efd78b89954d1b01b00037ece77df4c3844eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392718} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7f2c5a-e3a6-5cfd-9077-9d13a03ddc70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a33d5262-3afa-580b-b512-aba7c2576e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-50195" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int (x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/92/92a2dbaa5726e6a1ab83fa879ad7b630b05e68a8e366db214b74d85269a849a9.png)"}] [#uuid "a8e6c58c-da62-5dab-8325-a53c14d9b8ba" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096503 :question/id #uuid "a8e6c58c-da62-5dab-8325-a53c14d9b8ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-529" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not."}] [#uuid "ab40a4d3-c418-5b64-a04c-bb017155a4d2" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096504 :question/id #uuid "ab40a4d3-c418-5b64-a04c-bb017155a4d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-530" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $y'  = 2x,$ and sketch the solution curves of this equation." :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C.\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1.$\n\n![](images/e3/e37771799ef5fadc259e6ea0073042a83e0974b5a2619d1af3e87a7467e6d780.png)"}] [#uuid "af71cdb8-8b5c-5075-89d8-624e1e63c073" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe7d686-fceb-5893-8824-fa874f02fc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{x^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056209} {:answer/id #uuid "d39f9463-ac37-5073-9bd0-1c581c32bc5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "36x^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056210} {:answer/id #uuid "04d9595d-edc4-5313-ac43-3dca55608c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "12x^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056211} {:answer/id #uuid "10cd59df-624f-539a-877b-a604f947553a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{x^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056212} {:answer/id #uuid "249afd6c-aac5-5c78-b111-d1096dacdfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{x^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056213}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b3900d9b-b037-5571-8ce9-f4acb088f7bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056208}] :question/id #uuid "af71cdb8-8b5c-5075-89d8-624e1e63c073" :question/math-academy-id "q-10737" :question/problem "If $y = \\frac{6}{x^{6}}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."}] [#uuid "b551e489-4c37-53ea-831b-8f3ca007450b" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85752216-72d1-52c5-a307-1f7593bb2baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095431} {:answer/id #uuid "bffd2b2f-d555-5e17-8756-8a754febf526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095432} {:answer/id #uuid "eef892e3-6947-592f-9345-311b595e0536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095433} {:answer/id #uuid "05327ffd-b783-558f-8116-edad45453171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095434} {:answer/id #uuid "62baa9f7-a4b9-5ff4-84f6-37fdf189f3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095435}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a72fa786-3004-51a5-a233-17a9a216dfb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095430}] :question/id #uuid "b551e489-4c37-53ea-831b-8f3ca007450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48625" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (x^{4} + 3x)^{2}$."}] [#uuid "c6eeef64-0029-5193-805e-25131224aaa0" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e6e851-77d9-50d0-b99d-b8ca4b35fe00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349696} {:answer/id #uuid "bd11a2c4-956c-5921-bf71-eef3b4403859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350132} {:answer/id #uuid "d27ff332-32be-52d1-8f0c-1c58dea87e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350302} {:answer/id #uuid "ee885d63-62c6-5299-8069-ab09eb83e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350537} {:answer/id #uuid "fede89cf-752a-5d9b-a4c1-e5ef628a1c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350696}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350302} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71f2d30-074a-5e70-868b-367fb755a8c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c6eeef64-0029-5193-805e-25131224aaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8349" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] [#uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166659 :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [#uuid "c7fefc62-82f5-5578-9142-a780040dc0af" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abc22f1d-811f-5bdc-b37d-0282e04c8aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085095} {:answer/id #uuid "da5a2b38-cb05-5079-8a67-44b3023a6a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\ln x) = \\ln x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085096} {:answer/id #uuid "011ca4e6-3122-58c6-a064-72bf61a7cc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx^{2}}(yx^{2}) = x^{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085097} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc23e01-156d-58f7-838b-e84160a81d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{y}{x}) = \\frac{\\cos x}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085098} {:answer/id #uuid "6436279c-38d0-5c1d-a595-b7ab9c80521e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085099}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c0749d-dee0-5f31-b150-55643e10de7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085094}] :question/id #uuid "c7fefc62-82f5-5578-9142-a780040dc0af" :question/math-academy-id "q-17540" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos x$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."}] [#uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166657 :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"}] [#uuid "cb82aa2f-3e4a-523a-94c2-c95aa3cb8ffa" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a181c2-eaa7-54a0-8890-e935b4d4ff1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{2x}-2{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330021} {:answer/id #uuid "15c676e1-03b3-5352-b3b8-1386a7ac7b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330135} {:answer/id #uuid "c78be5e7-440c-5080-aede-7717891b5955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331721} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb467dd-d851-57e6-ba23-4d6c40542276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}-2{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331779} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0ac1c2-8887-5016-9ea9-5bc26155b0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{2x}-2{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330135} :answer-field/id #uuid "7491e487-3ec6-578a-921d-573211c3741a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb82aa2f-3e4a-523a-94c2-c95aa3cb8ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-50136" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{e}^{2x}=-2x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}y & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "d93ad997-2d1a-586a-bd5a-d470614a6ec6" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cdeff7-1bd1-52cc-b4d2-58765a1e715e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y = - (1)/(2(x - 1)) + \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094100} {:answer/id #uuid "e264582e-6aa3-5da8-b05d-657aca25a556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{x - 1} + \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094101} {:answer/id #uuid "43d2def0-e7d1-54fb-834f-84b6876ea10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x - 1} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094102} {:answer/id #uuid "518e0850-647a-5199-8686-13cfd746ca8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{x - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094103} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc7592f-e41a-5c1f-9369-d6bc76a6ea98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2}{x - 1} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094104}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd411f0-cb37-5900-866e-8862a9b0a1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094099}] :question/id #uuid "d93ad997-2d1a-586a-bd5a-d470614a6ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-119078" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(x - 1)^{2}y^{′} =-2$ that satisfies the condition $y(2) = 1$."}] [#uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087605 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"}] [#uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100731 :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [#uuid "e1c8faa6-71a3-52b0-9c4c-3e41ed01dd83" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f651d57-fd13-5223-b101-19044f5a3b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{11}{2}x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094688} {:answer/id #uuid "0c189129-a43b-5083-8426-a8d988b5bcfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{11}{2} - C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094689} {:answer/id #uuid "441b0a08-b772-5959-b466-9ce911e9e6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{11}{2}x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094690} {:answer/id #uuid "63128db0-6242-55ce-a386-75928939d9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{11}{2}x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094691} {:answer/id #uuid "7833225a-e7d7-58d8-a243-47f76b32d976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) = 11x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094692}] :answer-field/id #uuid "702c2b7a-1035-57b4-98fc-9ee1ec455ef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094687}] :question/id #uuid "e1c8faa6-71a3-52b0-9c4c-3e41ed01dd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-14245" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f^{′}(x) + 11x = 0$."}] [#uuid "e52f80d1-431d-51a0-9c51-950dc1a91065" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059447 :question/id #uuid "e52f80d1-431d-51a0-9c51-950dc1a91065" :question/math-academy-id "e-138" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 6x^3$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 3x^{2} \\\\\n&= 18x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401440} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82a4b2-58af-556c-a366-f92624768704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401441} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a26a57-987c-5668-b531-e2f3db2b75bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401442} {:answer/id #uuid "b6972414-a235-5987-855e-567ba571b605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401443} {:answer/id #uuid "e08cc03c-cea4-583d-b68b-e5c8622f76e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}=(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef647b19-79e2-5b54-b8df-1bf5fe9db9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"}] [#uuid "e8b9014e-32cf-5f04-856c-7fd663bea50f" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096502 :question/id #uuid "e8b9014e-32cf-5f04-856c-7fd663bea50f" :question/math-academy-id "e-528" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8dc75e-3cf3-5795-857d-b484a8bface7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348712} {:answer/id #uuid "43bb70a4-1ebc-568d-ac4e-2b15cf3ae1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349068} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0edda0-970f-571f-af28-8b2bb516b055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349843} {:answer/id #uuid "aa32fc93-bb6e-5db9-9787-457ed8b63eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349965} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e4844b-3007-5cd6-b780-6e847081831e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349068} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf81e7df-70d6-59a2-9d91-cf73151d372e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."}] [#uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0497b675-c4d7-5aec-bf1c-5d24dee89d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357800} {:answer/id #uuid "13713ac4-10e1-59ec-a786-cc04cf0b96f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357923} {:answer/id #uuid "23faf99c-a7e1-552f-8309-c422f0cf6b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358078} {:answer/id #uuid "732f6afd-9270-562c-a749-43bd8024a8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358759} {:answer/id #uuid "78ed70b7-8f6a-5447-93db-c6e70c7ee75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358806} :answer-field/id #uuid "da428f89-b735-5f26-9767-c7c21805d046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [#uuid "f61ac550-d62c-558f-b6d5-0d4c9aba8089" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :question/id #uuid "f61ac550-d62c-558f-b6d5-0d4c9aba8089" :question/math-academy-id "e-3629" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\log_3 x,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply our formula for the derivative when the base of the logarithm is a number other than $e.$ By doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 3}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{4}{x \\ln 3}.\n\n}\n$$"}] [#uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/math-academy-id "e-3626" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf'(x)      &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\n                            &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\n                            &=  3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln(2) \\\n                            &=  3 \\ln(2) \\cdot 2^{x}.\n\t}\n$$"}] [#uuid "17751a3b-ed4b-5966-a450-330460c02715" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a4235d-41c9-5a0e-b658-3ec6b027584e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398897} {:answer/id #uuid "4b317621-35b4-53d1-939d-fab67d8f1031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398898} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcf3415-556b-53c7-ac6f-21a5ddefd845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398899} {:answer/id #uuid "a552a9bb-4de8-5448-90ec-372d3554e55d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398900} {:answer/id #uuid "fa85c5fe-d852-5230-88fd-53d91e4bcd53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=C{x}^{2}+10x+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398898} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1789600-f409-5b68-8f83-26b29073f519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17751a3b-ed4b-5966-a450-330460c02715" :question/math-academy-id "q-50139" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-10.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-10\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-10x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-10\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D \\\\  & =-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [#uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096406 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}{)}^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059448 :question/id #uuid "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56" :question/math-academy-id "e-139" :question/problem "If $y=-\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "27b042e7-c453-5f49-bac9-de5985bbacde" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b595fdc-ac24-53b1-b485-072a76711f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/83/83c166a9882661ec123b3f14bf76c9c7585052d0e85e24c4e41b7bbde4de9326.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330637} {:answer/id #uuid "71cb7ed8-c659-56d9-95c6-9bd73867660f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/1e/1e746142e1dc26c247a6ac985c65072445658ad1f9b5ae298d246c1d81ab96bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330979} {:answer/id #uuid "7a615d72-2639-5796-9876-984ecf702dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/56/566935c224696f817a798a9e0f608ba384b5da6af1f0bfec6ad769e910f1230c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331039} {:answer/id #uuid "8feb98b8-4798-558b-9d2a-1181de6e3f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/4b/4bcb4f24caba3010cb8bfffecacbd392ad5951d4e42873d467c9007ad2b7ea46.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331233} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f5ab31-edff-5dee-88f0-7ee4a29422a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "images/1f/1fd9c2f2358171f102969930fde05347f50bd497f725fdc4c24fec97490f15ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330637} :answer-field/id #uuid "f42fe6aa-d5ce-5802-8ca2-328c4a548b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27b042e7-c453-5f49-bac9-de5985bbacde" :question/math-academy-id "q-50196" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-3(x+1)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-3\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,1.$\n\n![](images/03/03a5bf5134dc7d551b2602145c4c25081a2ad274f02c95845e5789b288e618a1.png)"}] [#uuid "29a0265e-ad88-5ac9-88fe-878aa4f0d0bf" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096505 :question/id #uuid "29a0265e-ad88-5ac9-88fe-878aa4f0d0bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-531" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$$\nf'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0) = 2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ 2 & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2.\n$$"}] [#uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087606 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."}] [#uuid "36f54028-9879-56b6-bec9-c97cd80fb58e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9acd2481-0ec3-5994-9168-964cb3c474c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}e^{x} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095671} {:answer/id #uuid "b3631b1e-e008-5fb9-a825-84c8ecba10b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) = e^{x} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095672} {:answer/id #uuid "c508b657-4c25-56c5-960c-217135f76315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{1}{2}e^{x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095673} {:answer/id #uuid "f83fba29-574c-5d24-b1a6-4adbe9283fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095674} {:answer/id #uuid "38b83252-dae4-5abd-841f-1818a0da90fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "f(x) =-\\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095675}] :answer-field/id #uuid "62dcfac4-1b46-5254-bc04-921bedd65cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095670}] :question/id #uuid "36f54028-9879-56b6-bec9-c97cd80fb58e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49942" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0) =-1$.\n\n$2f^{′}(x) + e^{x} = 0$."}] [#uuid "3e04bc45-f34f-56fc-9a0e-8643597c4852" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae67e9eb-7a6a-5f1f-9633-a869be11af9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398903} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9d1539-56f7-56d2-9de6-1e9901343c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398904} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9ac46a-1dd6-5423-b497-fd6f6d213a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398905} {:answer/id #uuid "bd19773a-598c-5d4a-9935-ec0892a47ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398906} {:answer/id #uuid "89be7220-c09b-594d-8bff-c3a06f0b9221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398905} :answer-field/id #uuid "30eeac9f-d833-5bc8-934f-4097337092f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e04bc45-f34f-56fc-9a0e-8643597c4852" :question/math-academy-id "q-50148" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{3x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [#uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50433ce6-5f47-50dd-9e9b-85d17224a715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358447} {:answer/id #uuid "7227653e-d62c-513d-9a69-bdac1666e341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358754} {:answer/id #uuid "b29e28c8-4bf6-5d40-8c9d-8c40c5abc4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359311} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c5eecb-38a5-5274-a5bd-d4d7a88d8192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359368} {:answer/id #uuid "dbea906c-0509-5763-971e-c278d2b7e4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359311} :answer-field/id #uuid "c723b3f2-2a27-58ff-a975-f834b8df18f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e9b8880-609b-5a27-b50e-083098d2cbea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95984" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [#uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \n                          f'(x)             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^{4} - x^2 \\right)\\\n                                             &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x\\right)+\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3x^{4}\\right) -\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\n\t\t\t\t\t     &= 4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right)+3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^{4}\\right) - 2x \\\t\t\t\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  3 \\cdot 4x^{3} - 2x \\\n                                             &=  4 e^x+  12x^{3} - 2x.\n}\n$$"}] [#uuid "4a354b54-aabb-5908-97e9-b4409b8eca72" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a944189-6813-5164-879c-eab76a5beda0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "12x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058189} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc3a755-7484-5e4e-9aa0-2019d5c8e3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "12x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058190} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc4c54-58ba-50c8-bfe6-6e8e10018dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "24x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058191} {:answer/id #uuid "6b798069-ae3e-55a5-aef7-730100f68aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "7x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058192} {:answer/id #uuid "76130876-a748-5eaa-b66a-f6da481d85b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\frac{4x^{4}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058193}] :answer-field/id #uuid "be302e32-8941-5ade-84be-3b85b8b0d362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058188}] :question/id #uuid "4a354b54-aabb-5908-97e9-b4409b8eca72" :question/math-academy-id "q-272" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 4x^{3}$."}] [#uuid "58d47fb0-cf75-58c4-8eea-f3ae97333159" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :question/id #uuid "58d47fb0-cf75-58c4-8eea-f3ae97333159" :question/math-academy-id "e-3628" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2\\ln x+x^2-1$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) &= \\left(2\\ln x+x^2-1\\right)'\\\n\t\t\t&=2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}+2x-0\\\n\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{2}{x}+2x.\n\n}\n$$"}] [#uuid "5eaa00f8-0fd0-5de6-a7c0-965db6de3a5c" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :question/id #uuid "5eaa00f8-0fd0-5de6-a7c0-965db6de3a5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3630" :question/problem "Given that $y= \\log_4 x + \\ln x$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\log_4 x + \\ln x \\right)\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\log_4 x) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\ln x )\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\dfrac{1}{x}.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{1}{xln⁡4}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ln⁡4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [#uuid "6624681a-bb00-59db-8df9-4d5a3cd00434" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "6624681a-bb00-59db-8df9-4d5a3cd00434" :question/math-academy-id "e-3627" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\ln x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}   & = \\left(5\\ln x\\right)'\\  \n                                                    & = 5 (\\ln x)'\\\n                                                    &=5\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}\\\n                                                    &= \\dfrac{5}{x}.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t  \n\n}\n$$"}] [#uuid "6d51dc01-44b0-56ff-973b-78a9457915f0" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88cdd62d-36a3-56b4-ad53-5ef4ae1b675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "36x^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056395} 